<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Festival Season.</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Look at the Fireworks!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Use visual information in texts to convey meaning. Communication: Explain simple written instructions to peers, using English or Spanish.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use visual information to clarify responses about everyday objects (e.g., points to cotton candy for "festival food"). Can explain simple written instructions to peers by pointing to pictures (e.g., points to a festival picture and says "festival"). Can describe festival traditions using gestures (e.g., clapping hands for "drums").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e0e0;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 60%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads students in a "Festival Flashcard Dance." The teacher shows flashcards of festival items (fireworks, cotton candy, music, dance, drums). When a flashcard is shown, students say the word and make a gesture (e.g., clap for "drums," pretend to eat for "cotton candy," wiggle for "dance"). This activates prior vocabulary and connects to the theme and scenario.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a "fireworks" flashcard and says, "Look! Fireworks! I like the fireworks! They are bright!" (pointing to the card and making a hand gesture like an exploding firework). Then holds up "cotton candy" and says, "This is cotton candy. I like to eat cotton candy. It is fun!" (pointing and making an eating gesture). The teacher consistently uses the target grammar (Subject + verb + adjective) and vocabulary while pointing to visuals and using gestures to convey meaning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple questions and visuals.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of fireworks) "Is this quiet or loud?" (Expected answer: "Loud!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to cotton candy) "Do I eat this or watch this?" (Expected answer: "Eat this!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large, simple "Festival Celebration Poster" created by the teacher as an example. The poster has 3-4 clear illustrations of festival activities (e.g., fireworks, eating cotton candy, playing drums) with very short, simple sentences next to each (e.g., "Fireworks! They are bright!", "Eat cotton candy! It is fun!", "Play drums! It is loud!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Cultural awareness: Talking about and showing respect for different festivals (e.g., "I like Fiestas Patrias and I like Halloween.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher points to different parts of the example poster and asks students to identify the pictures and explain simple actions or descriptions using gestures and pointing.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to fireworks on poster) "What is this? Is it fun?" (Expected: "Fireworks! Yes, it is fun!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to cotton candy) "What do I like to eat?" (Expected: "Cotton candy!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a child playing drums) "Clap your hands for drums!" (Students clap, demonstrating understanding of the action and word).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Subject + verb + adjective for descriptive sentences (e.g., "It is fun!"), Present simple (e.g., "I like the meal."), WH-questions (e.g., "What do you like?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> festival, fireworks, meal, shaved ice, cotton candy, music, dance, drums, fun, happy, loud, bright, colorful.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /f/ in "fireworks", /m/ in "meal", P /p/ "party".</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Visual Matching for Meaning:</b> The teacher provides students with a worksheet containing two columns: one with pictures of festival items (e.g., fireworks, cotton candy, drums, music) and another with simple descriptive sentences or action words (e.g., "It is bright!", "Eat this!", "Clap hands!", "Dance!"). Students draw lines to match the pictures to the correct sentences/words. The teacher circulates, asking "What is this?" and "What do you do?" while pointing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Picture Explanation Pairs:</b> Students work in pairs with sets of small festival pictures and pre-printed sentence frames (e.g., "I like the _____. It is _____."). Student A picks a picture (e.g., fireworks), shows it to Student B, and says, "I like the fireworks. They are bright!" while pointing to the picture. Student B then picks a different picture (e.g., cotton candy) and says, "I like the cotton candy. It is fun!" using gestures and pointing to explain their picture. The teacher encourages explaining meaning through visuals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Tell Me What I Do!" Game:</b> The teacher shows a flashcard of a festival item or activity (e.g., "dance," "eat," "clap"). Students, in pairs, must explain to each other what the flashcard means, using gestures and simple words, without saying the word on the card. For example, if the card is "dance," a student might move their body and say, "We move our body! It is fun!" or "I like this!" (Mediation: using non-verbal cues and simple language to convey the instruction).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students present their "Festival Celebration Poster" (Project 1) to a small group of peers. They use simple English sentences, point to their illustrations, and use gestures to explain their favorite festival activities, including food and fun, effectively mediating the information from their poster to their peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Small group sharing (3-4 students per group).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Poster Preparation:</b> Students ensure their "Festival Celebration Poster" (which they started creating in previous lessons) is complete with illustrations and simple sentences (e.g., "I like fireworks. They are bright. I like cotton candy. It is fun!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Group Presentation:</b> Students sit in small groups. One by one, each student holds up their poster and explains it to their group. They must use at least two sentences to describe their favorite festival items, pointing to the pictures as they speak. For example, "Look at my poster! This is fireworks! I like the fireworks! They are bright!" (pointing to fireworks). "This is shaved ice. I like shaved ice! It is yummy!" (pointing to shaved ice).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Interaction / Clarification:</b> Group members are encouraged to ask simple questions like "What is this?" (pointing to a picture on a peer's poster). The presenter then mediates the answer by pointing to the picture and stating the word or a simple explanation (e.g., "It is music! You can dance!"). They can also explain a simple instruction (e.g., "Eat the corn!" while pointing).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during small group presentations. The teacher circulates and notes individual student participation and application of mediation skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Festival Celebration Poster" to their small group, explaining at least two festival items and one simple instruction. They must use pointing and gestures to clarify their meaning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., /f/ in "fireworks", /m/ in "meal", /p/ in "party").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least 3 target vocabulary words (e.g., festival, fireworks, cotton candy, fun, bright, eat, watch).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanations using visuals and gestures (points to poster items, uses gestures for actions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to explain a simple instruction (e.g., "Watch the fireworks!", "Eat the meal!").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with two prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can show my poster and say what I like. (Circle: Yes / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">My favorite thing on my poster is _________.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was your favorite part of making and sharing your festival poster?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was it easy or difficult to explain your poster to your friends using English words and pointing? What helped you?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the lesson effectively supported students in mediating meaning through visuals and gestures? Did students successfully use visual information to convey meaning and explain simple instructions?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did students integrate the target vocabulary and grammar when explaining their posters? Were there any common pronunciation challenges (e.g., /f/, /m/ sounds)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds might be needed for future mediation tasks with 2nd graders, especially regarding clarifying instructions or describing items?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I further encourage student-to-student interaction and the use of English during collaborative project sharing?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Festival Season" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme, possibly focusing on "Animals and their Homes," building on the acquired vocabulary and sentence structures while introducing new ones. Students will continue to practice using visuals and simple language to understand and convey meaning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>